#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "ef6bfc82-5993-514e-bbb4-6b3769bec0e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-752" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=e^{-x}$ and $a=3.$\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ f(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-{e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =-{e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-3}+(-{e}^{-3})(x-3)+\\frac{({e}^{-3})}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(-{e}^{-3})}{3!}(x-3)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}-{e}^{-3}(x-3)+\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{2}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{6}(x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "e82fb0f9-372a-50ab-a6a5-c1dc8868a2d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-753" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9.1} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(9.1-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(9.1-9)^{2} \\\\  & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(0.1)-\\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.016\\,620,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places. \n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc20135-5b7b-5eae-bfff-0f0a1960fe20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2}=1+(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(x+1)^{4}}{4}+\\frac{(x+1)^{6}}{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315094} {:answer/id #uuid "5742a0c4-23ba-5b1f-a23f-5b9e10b074dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2}=2+(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315095} {:answer/id #uuid "55ea2b50-1604-5d5c-bdbf-c518681a2d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2}=1-(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}-(x+1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315096} {:answer/id #uuid "51984cf6-87df-5419-bba7-fa0acef73b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2}=2-(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{4}-(x+1)^{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315097} {:answer/id #uuid "f46eaf94-cc1f-5b2a-bf1a-e3a51c7a0a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2}=1-(x+1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55ea2b50-1604-5d5c-bdbf-c518681a2d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2}=1-(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}-(x+1)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315096} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e02c16f-1538-5865-b304-ecc4154e5812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb65a227-175b-5c42-aa58-2cfefad23b3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126846" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+2}$ about the point $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ f(-1) & =(-1+2)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1+2)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =2(-1+2)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-6(x+2)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-1) & =-6(-1+2)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =1+(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+1)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}-(x+1)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c241ca2c-bbf5-5377-99ef-435cbc377753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315101} {:answer/id #uuid "567f12c8-c922-5d6d-a930-99483ea9ef1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.421" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315102} {:answer/id #uuid "9741e3d2-fd7b-5558-b19b-bdf48cbd60cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315103} {:answer/id #uuid "2f228185-df63-5c8b-98a0-43513296937c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315104} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a8e6d-716f-51cb-a80f-fd19f36cadd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a8e6d-716f-51cb-a80f-fd19f36cadd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315105} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e2e4c4f-5dd7-5f99-a0fe-b40a225fa0bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56b858e8-c4fa-5c2c-84ef-c3854e0367d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126875" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ about $x=0$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=0.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ f(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{0+1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{(0+1)^{2}}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(0+1)^{3}}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =(0)+(1)(x-0)+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-0)^{2}+\\frac{(2)}{3!}(x-0)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=0.5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.5 & ≈(0.5)-\\frac{1}{2}(0.5)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(0.5)^{3} \\\\  & =0.417\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63eb126b-998b-5738-99d3-79c8d12d0f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.739" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315108} {:answer/id #uuid "6e983fbc-7a3b-5d32-ba55-edc40db4982c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315109} {:answer/id #uuid "857b6e71-5737-5493-8f07-a81600361b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.744" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315110} {:answer/id #uuid "f402e085-7c9e-5902-84fa-aa694e9b204f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.746" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315111} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffed095-ed97-5bd4-9101-ccf3484fc367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.732" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e983fbc-7a3b-5d32-ba55-edc40db4982c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315109} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3b40202-1b04-576e-97dc-7280df173f35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fcccafb-96d2-5248-a5bc-581af0a17196" :question/math-academy-id "q-126877" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1+(2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(4)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈1+2(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})+2{(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{200} \\\\  & =0.735\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0038b334-e6d3-5ea6-92f7-e9cc7fc417a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0038b334-e6d3-5ea6-92f7-e9cc7fc417a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310616} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c075d1a-08c8-5a2f-9124-b9d5609b9985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f2c13f1-81f7-545f-882e-aa6e418d6c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-347635" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{1.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df544f64-1e00-53a5-89db-2ca6d853187a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df544f64-1e00-53a5-89db-2ca6d853187a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315115} :answer-field/id #uuid "1879832a-7d6c-552b-99ef-a9f7a9e2cf67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315114} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "459e56fa-7c62-58b5-b02b-4953cb089f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "459e56fa-7c62-58b5-b02b-4953cb089f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315117} :answer-field/id #uuid "942da90c-4c11-5738-994e-55674e808b10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315116} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31ad8cbf-3b36-545d-a4dd-44bdc8d8ba49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31ad8cbf-3b36-545d-a4dd-44bdc8d8ba49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315119} :answer-field/id #uuid "19a200f6-6b83-5ee7-a15a-cb8cd15b2fcd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "412cac86-33d7-57b1-9ed4-099cebbff26a" :question/math-academy-id "q-353748" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ about the point $x=1.$\n\n$\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=${{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}-⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{-4}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}=-4{x}^{-5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-4(1)^{-5}=-4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{20}{{x}^{6}}=20{x}^{-6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =20(1)^{-6}=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} & =1+(-4)(x-1)+\\frac{20}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(-120)}{3!}(x-1)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4(x-1)+10(x-1)^{2}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a3b73c8-441e-5595-af0a-40fc3c0044a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a3b73c8-441e-5595-af0a-40fc3c0044a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310619} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e2ac35-9b2d-5677-9538-808448878cfc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b364c5d0-46c3-5c92-834b-0849c46c5e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b364c5d0-46c3-5c92-834b-0849c46c5e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310621} :answer-field/id #uuid "771391b4-e95f-5fed-9c1d-e057532cc808" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d889759e-ad5c-52fb-8505-431f6294d5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d889759e-ad5c-52fb-8505-431f6294d5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310623} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6a6a64-1c09-5353-bd0d-458d03cc46f5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81f76b8b-d322-5853-848e-fee21cd6de31" :question/math-academy-id "q-353904" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5b4f0c-a8d4-5b33-8279-eaa99e44613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b5b4f0c-a8d4-5b33-8279-eaa99e44613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310626} :answer-field/id #uuid "f396c657-75b7-585a-b01c-dda57dfb6953" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310625} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16752fcf-1e06-52cf-b22f-480f0d871686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16752fcf-1e06-52cf-b22f-480f0d871686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310628} :answer-field/id #uuid "70011fbc-ce42-5c2c-9f80-987048adb492" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310627} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f2aa15e-2678-5509-85d1-d0e9b5d84fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f2aa15e-2678-5509-85d1-d0e9b5d84fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310630} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc5fdec-6177-53d9-87fe-40b399c35de6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ba4e3d-1327-530e-8993-de1be5288167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ba4e3d-1327-530e-8993-de1be5288167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310632} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c4be7e-dc44-57f7-90b0-9cdc7c2de488" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f93c24-fffd-543d-8271-aa03ba162e9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353905" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ about $x=1.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-4}}\n$(x-1)^{3}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =1+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{-1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f7733a-e203-5955-a0c4-4651f483748d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1+2(x+2)+3(x+2)^{2}+6(x+2)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315122} {:answer/id #uuid "4769134a-6e2d-5876-ba70-e6374cc2431f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315123} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1e193b-b2cd-570e-9408-725025ccf94b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315124} {:answer/id #uuid "46a98f0c-90ce-5056-b47d-e43ad80d0bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315125} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb24355-b6fc-5261-a43e-938374eab8b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-\\frac{(x+2)}{2}+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4769134a-6e2d-5876-ba70-e6374cc2431f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315123} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c71e486-aef9-537f-8515-b3787190972b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7afe118-2a0b-56b6-9c4b-c1f500a7e109" :question/math-academy-id "q-83185" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+3}$ about the point $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+3}=(x+3)^{-1} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+3)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=-(x+3)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =-(-2+3)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+3)^{3}}=2(x+3)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =2(-2+3)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x+3)^{4}}=-6(x+3)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =-6(-2+3)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3} & =1+(-1)(x+2)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f45b9f-8f31-5874-838b-ad7e19edb0ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{19}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315129} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6ae820-3a19-590c-8709-2f75d4e37241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{17}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315130} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bb2de3-dfa7-5cc3-b432-19f9fef8b71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{8}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315131} {:answer/id #uuid "808444ec-1ec7-5d3f-a798-b0a0aee0390d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315132} {:answer/id #uuid "c83d3f79-7368-58d1-bda0-c3a7bf3a7458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44f45b9f-8f31-5874-838b-ad7e19edb0ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{19}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315129} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0192f89-92ee-5f28-a896-029429db6eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f750b509-3c63-5fc0-8993-9bde234b6a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83217" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{-1/2}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of ${8}^{-1/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ f(9) & ={9}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(9)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1/2} & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}(x-9)+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x-9}{54}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=8$ into the above to approximate ${8}^{-1/2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{-1/2} & ≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{8-9}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{54}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcad847-55c5-54c9-978e-aab990d0c5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183846} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e420be-7458-5309-8fa6-53d19346dcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183847} {:answer/id #uuid "226bcbbc-cfb5-56a0-801f-07bdeefada77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183848} {:answer/id #uuid "343b2896-72d4-5135-9e03-fd07955dee6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183849} {:answer/id #uuid "70bd5fc4-279e-5b7a-a253-acc7b5e9554c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16439" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "343b2896-72d4-5135-9e03-fd07955dee6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183849} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc495eb-3490-5a68-b1d6-e8a03d5ba5b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa3dd46a-8d87-5019-a6c8-488757b3b77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9608" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(10-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{216} \\\\  & =3.162\\,04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places."}]}
